  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>The math program has {{ program.lessons.length }} lessons in three levels and a bridge into second grade, with a mastery test after every tenth lesson and a placement test to find where a child should begin. It covers the number, operations, measurement, data, and geometry skills of the Common Core standards for Kindergarten and First Grade, and the mathematics of the Head Start Early Learning Outcomes Framework for Pre-K.</p>
  <p>It is built on Direct Instruction, the method developed by Siegfried Engelmann. Each new step is shown, done together, and then done alone. Errors are corrected the same way every time. Skills are taught in tracks that run side by side, and each is reviewed at widening intervals until it is permanent.</p>

  <h2>Why it was built</h2>
  <p>Many children struggle with early math not because it is hard but because it was taught unclearly or out of order. Direct Instruction removes the guesswork. Teach Direct gives a parent at home the same careful sequence, scripts, and checks that a well-run classroom program provides.</p>
  <p>It has no games, points, or prizes. The aim is for the child to learn the mathematics and to feel the quiet confidence that comes from knowing it. The child sees only pictures and numbers on the screen; you read every word.</p>

  <h2>Who made it</h2>
  <p>Teach Direct is built and maintained by <a href="https://www.pikespeakwebdesigns.com">Pikes Peak Web Designs</a>. The source code is on <a href="https://github.com/jonajinga/math-direct">GitHub</a>, under the MIT License. Corrections and suggestions are welcome.</p>

